Falsifies `kernel::arch::aarch64::iommu::proofs::the_smmu_is_handed_exactly_the_tables_the_kernel_built`.

The defect is a one-character error in a shift, in the exact shape splitting a 64-bit
physical address across two 32-bit words invites: the SMMU's context descriptor carries
TTB0 in bits [51:4] of dword 1, so the high word is `ttb >> 32`, and this writes
`ttb >> 31`. Bit 31 of the table root is duplicated into bit 32 of the address the SMMU
walks, so on a machine whose page tables live above 2 GiB the IOMMU translates every one
of that device's transactions through some other frame entirely. Not a fault, not a
crash: confinement pointed somewhere wrong.

**Every test in this tree stays green under this patch**, which is the reason the harness
is worth its place. QEMU's `virt` board puts RAM at `0x4000_0000` and the domain roots the
confinement test allocates sit around there with bit 31 clear, so `>> 31` and `>> 32`
agree on this board and `the_iommu_faults_a_dma_that_escapes_the_domain` passes exactly as
it did before. The witness set is the addresses with bit 31 set, and nobody writes those
down; the solver enumerates them.

Expected to fail: `assert!(ttb_dword == ttb, "the CD's TTB0 is the table root entire, high
half included")`. Confirmed red on 2026-09-04.

The sibling harness `no_stream_can_reach_another_streams_tables` stays green under this
patch, which is the honest half of the record: it is about `table_offset` and never calls
`context_descriptor`.

